1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,050
The Speed of Light.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:12,412
The speed of light in a vacuum, 299,792,458 meters per second, is a fundamental constant denoted c.

3
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:22,550
This value is exact because the International System of Units defines the meter as the distance light travels in 1/299,792,458 of a second.

4
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:48,525
Astronomical distances illustrate this scale: light from the Moon reaches Earth in 1.3 seconds; sunlight takes 8 minutes and 20 seconds to traverse 150 million kilometers; light from Proxima Centauri requires 4.25 years; and photons from the Andromeda Galaxy travel for 2.5 million years before detection — meaning terrestrial telescopes observe that galaxy as it existed before Homo sapiens appeared.

5
00:00:48,525 --> 00:01:01,725
Einstein's special theory of relativity, published in 1905, established two profound consequences: the speed of light is invariant across all inertial reference frames, and no massive object can reach c.

6
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:10,062
As an object's velocity approaches light speed, relativistic mass approaches infinity, requiring infinite energy to accelerate further.

7
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:20,900
Time dilation causes moving clocks to tick slower relative to stationary observers — a prediction confirmed by comparing atomic clocks on high-speed aircraft with ground-based counterparts.

8
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:42,137
The Global Positioning System must correct for both special relativistic time dilation (satellites moving at 14,000 km/h) and general relativistic gravitational time dilation (weaker gravity at 20,200 km altitude); without these relativity corrections, positioning errors would accumulate at roughly 10 kilometers per day.

9
00:01:42,137 --> 00:01:52,612
The speed of light represents the cosmic speed limit for causality: no information or physical effect can propagate faster than c, preserving cause-and-effect structure in spacetime.

10
00:01:52,612 --> 00:02:01,787
Quantum entanglement produces correlations that appear instantaneous, but the no-communication theorem proves entanglement alone cannot transmit information.

11
00:02:01,787 --> 00:02:10,587
Only massless particles — photons and gluons — travel at c; neutrinos have small but nonzero mass and move slightly slower.

12
00:02:10,587 --> 00:02:23,037
Gravitational waves propagate at exactly c, confirmed by LIGO's 2017 detection of a neutron star merger observed in both gravitational waves and electromagnetic radiation simultaneously.

